# dawn
## The sixth
They come in by the barrier of the Étoile at about seven in the evening, in the dusk, in a rain that has begun again, a procession of sixty thousand. It is made of the Guard, and the women, and the carts of flour, and the cannon, and the Gardes du corps with their hats on pikes, and in the middle of it, in a coach with the blinds up and the lamps lit, there is a king, and a queen, and a child, and a sister, in the light of ten thousand torches. They sing. The women march round the coach with their arms linked. They have put green boughs in their hats. They have loaves on the ends of their pikes. They are crying, to the whole of Paris, that they have the baker, and the baker's wife, and the baker's boy.
It is the end of Versailles. You hear it said in the crowd, in a dozen voices, and you do not think that anyone says it twice. They take the king to the Tuileries. It is an empty old palace, where nobody has lived for a century, and the beds are not made.
?if b3.versailles == "door"
  A young guardsman with a bandage on his mouth has been put at the foot of the stair. He sees you in the crowd. He does not say anything. He raises his hand.
?elif b3.versailles == "women"
  You have walked a long way in the rain beside a loaf on a pike. It is a thing that you are not going to be able to explain to the Senate.
?end
?if b3.vault == "broke"
  There is a letter on the counter when you come in. It is not in the Prince's hand. It is in Ottaviano's, which is very small, and it is only one line. It says that she has not woken, but that on the second night after, at about the hour of the nones, she moved her left hand, as well.
?elif b3.vault == "read"
  You think, for a long time, of the sentence at the foot of the slate. There is a good deal in it that you cannot take in. There is one thing that you cannot put away. It is the word bread.
?else
  You do not think of the third cellar. It is a thing that you have put, with some care, at the back of the mind, with a key turned on it. It will be there.
?end
The Prince goes to the Tuileries behind the king, in a plain carriage, by the Pont Royal, with his household and a good many trunks. There is a small gray face at the window. It is looking at nothing. It is the face of a man who has just been told what he is going to do, in his own writing.
$ do rest()
=> next
